insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d09558db-650a-5eef-a3cc-7d5ab167ec1a', 'wg10-argument-writing-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$A Weather Station for Calder High$q$, $q$(1) Calder High School's earth science classes study weather almost entirely from textbook charts and data collected hundreds of miles away. (2) Weather is something that affects everyone every day. (3) The science department should install a rooftop weather station so that students can collect and analyze local data themselves. (4) A basic station measures temperature, humidity, wind speed and rainfall, and it uploads readings every ten minutes to a free online classroom portal. (5) At nearby Fairlow High, which installed a similar station two years ago, the average score on the regional earth science exam rose from 71 to 79 percent. (6) Fairlow's teachers reported that students asked more questions when the numbers came from their own neighborhood. (7) Fairlow also has a very large parking lot that was repaved last summer.

(8) Some members of the school board argue that the station's cost of about $2,400 is too high when the department already struggles to replace broken microscopes. (9) That concern deserves a serious answer. (10) The Calder Parent Association has offered to cover half the cost through its spring fundraiser, and the station's sensors carry a ten-year warranty. (11) Spread across a decade, the district's share would come to roughly $120 a year, less than the price of a single new microscope.

(12) The station would also support classes beyond earth science. (13) Math students could graph a month of temperature readings, and the journalism club could publish weekly weather reports for the school website. (14) So yeah, a weather station is a really good idea and people should stop complaining about it.$q$, 'draft')
