-- Passage wg10-argument-writing-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('78f1f424-df33-5f4d-ae2b-9e30d8b747a8', 'wg10-argument-writing-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Voices of Westvale$q$, $q$(1) Every spring, sophomores at Westvale High write a research paper on a historical event using only books and websites. (2) This year the history department should replace that paper with an oral history project in which each student interviews an older resident about a local event. (3) Oral history asks students to do what historians actually do: gather firsthand accounts, compare them with written records and explain where they differ. (4) When the Westvale Historical Society piloted the project with one class last year, students recorded 26 interviews about the 1987 flood that closed the Marsh Road bridge for eight months. (5) Several of those recordings described evacuation routes that appeared in no newspaper from the time. (6) The interviews are now stored in the society's archive, where future researchers can use them.

(7) Some teachers worry that the project would weaken students' research writing skills. (8) Students in the pilot class still wrote a five-page paper analyzing their interview alongside at least three written sources. (9) Their average writing score on the department rubric was 3.4 out of 4, slightly higher than the 3.2 average in classes that wrote the traditional paper.

(10) Other teachers point out that some students may struggle to find someone to interview. (11) The historical society has offered a list of more than sixty volunteers who are willing to be interviewed at the public library. (12) The society's building on Pike Street used to be a general store. (13) Honestly, it's kind of obvious this project is better.$q$, 'draft')
